ent 7: The squirrel visits the mouse. So, squirrel's action is visiting the mouse.\n\nStatement 8: The squirrel visits the tiger. So, squirrel also visits the tiger.\n\nStatement 9: The tiger is kind. So, tiger's trait is kind.\n\nStatement 10: The tiger is nice. So, tiger's trait is nice.\n\nStatement 11: If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse is nice. So, visiting the mouse implies the mouse is nice.\n\nFrom statement 7, the squirrel visits the mouse. So, according to statement 11, the mouse is nice. So, mouse is nice.\n\nWait, but statement 3 says the mouse is kind. So, mouse is both kind and nice now.\n\nStatement 12: If someone eats the mouse and they visit the tiger, then the mouse is green. So, if someone eats the mouse and visits the tiger, then mouse is green.\n\nFrom statement 1, the lion eats the mouse. So, the lion is the one who eats the mouse. Does the lion visit the tiger? Let's see.\n\nStatement 15: If the lion is nice, then the lion does not visit the tiger. But we don't know if the lion is nice yet.\n\nWait, statement 10 says the tiger is nice. So, tiger is nice. But what about the lion? We don't have any statement that says the lion is nice or not. So, maybe the lion isn't nice.\n\nBut let's see. Statement 17: If someone is green, then they visit the tiger. The lion is green (statement 2), so according to statement 17, the lion visits the tiger.\n\nSo, the lion is green, so lion visits the tiger. So, the lion does visit the tiger.\n\nBut statement 15 says if the lion is nice, then it doesn't visit the tiger. But we just concluded that the lion does visit the tiger because it's green. So, if the lion visits the tiger, then the condition in statement 15 is that if the lion is nice, it doesn't visit the tiger. But since the lion does visit the tiger, that would mean the lion is not nice. Because if the lion were nice, it wouldn't visit the tiger, but it does, so the lion must not be nice.\n\nSo, lion is not nice.\n\nNow, going back to statement 12: If someone eats the mouse and they visit the tiger, then the mouse is green. The lion eats the mouse (statement 1) and visits the tiger (from statement 17). So, both conditions are met. Therefore, the mouse is green.\n\nSo, mouse is green.\n\nFrom statement 14: If the mouse is green, then the mouse is big. So, since the mouse is green, it's big.\n\nSo, mouse is big.\n\nNow, statement 13: Big, nice people are round. Wait, but the mouse is big and we know the mouse is nice (from statement 11, because the squirrel visits the mouse, so mouse is nice). So, mouse is big and nice, so according to statement 13, the mouse is round.\n\nSo, mouse is round.\n\nNow, statement 16: If someone is round and big, then they like the lion. The mouse is round and big, so according to statement 16, the mouse likes the lion.\n\nSo, the mouse likes the lion.\n\nBut the question is whether the mouse does not like the lion. So, if the mouse likes the lion, then the statement \"The mouse does not like the lion\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check to make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nLet me recap:\n\n- Lion eats mouse (1)\n- Lion is green (2)\n- Mouse is kind (3)\n- Squirrel is green (4)\n- Squirrel is round (5)\n- Squirrel likes lion (6)\n- Squirrel visits mouse (7) \u2192 mouse is nice (11)\n- Squirrel visits tiger (8)\n- Tiger is kind (9)\n- Tiger is nice (10)\n- Lion is green \u2192 visits tiger (17)\n- Lion visits tiger \u2192 since lion is green, so yes (17)\n- Lion is not nice because if it were nice, it wouldn't visit tiger (15)\n- Lion eats mouse and visits tiger \u2192 mouse is green (12)\n- Mouse is green \u2192 mouse is big (14)\n- Mouse is big and nice \u2192 mouse is round (13)\n- Mouse is round and big \u2192 likes lion (16)\n\nSo, all the deductions lead to the mouse liking the lion. Therefore, the statement \"The mouse does not like the lion\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any contradiction or if I missed any condition.\n\nAnother angle: Does the mouse like the lion? From statement 16, yes, because mouse is round and big, so it likes the lion.\n\nIs there any statement that says the mouse doesn't like the lion? I don't think so. All the deductions point to the mouse liking the lion.\n\nSo, the answer should be B) False, because the statement is false; the mouse does like the lion.\n</think>\n\nThe mouse is deduced to like the lion based on the given statements, making the statement \"The mouse does not like the lion\" false.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
