t the mouse. But does the mouse chase the cat? Statement 9 says the mouse does not chase the cow, but nothing about chasing the cat. So, the mouse might or might not chase the cat.\n\nWait, but statement 16 says if something is round, then it chases the cat. The mouse is round (statement 11), so according to statement 16, the mouse chases the cat. So, the mouse chases the cat.\n\nIf the mouse chases the cat, then from statement 15, the cat eats the mouse. So, the cat would eat the mouse if the mouse chases it.\n\nBut from statement 14, if something chases the cat, then it does not chase the mouse. So, if the mouse chases the cat, then the mouse does not chase the mouse. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Let me clarify.\n\nWait, statement 14 says if something chases the cat, then it does not chase the mouse. So, if the mouse chases the cat, then the mouse does not chase the mouse. But the mouse can't chase itself, right? So, that's a bit of a paradox, but perhaps it's just a way to say that if the mouse is chasing the cat, it's not chasing the mouse (which is itself, so that's a bit nonsensical, but maybe it's just a way to structure the logic).\n\nBut more importantly, if the mouse chases the cat, then from statement 15, the cat eats the mouse. So, the cat would eat the mouse if the mouse chases it.\n\nBut from statement 16, since the mouse is round, it chases the cat. So, the mouse does chase the cat. Therefore, the cat would eat the mouse.\n\nNow, does the cat chase itself? Let's see.\n\nIf the cat were to chase itself, that would mean the cat is chasing the cat. But from statement 14, if something chases the cat, then it does not chase the mouse. So, if the cat is chasing itself, then the cat does not chase the mouse. But does the cat chase the mouse?\n\nFrom the given statements, I don't see any direct statement about the cat chasing the mouse. However, statement 15 says if the mouse chases the cat, then the cat eats the mouse. But the mouse does chase the cat (fro